WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.847
An argument array provides little
protection if the request can choose which program

2
00:00:04.927 --> 00:00:05.591
receives it.

3
00:00:05.671 --> 00:00:09.716
Executable selection should come from
application code or trusted deployment

4
00:00:09.796 --> 00:00:10.641
configuration.

5
00:00:10.721 --> 00:00:13.136
When an endpoint supports several
operations,

6
00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:16.959
accept a logical operation key and map it
to a closed set of approved tools.

7
00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:18.126
Reject unknown keys.

8
00:00:18.206 --> 00:00:22.493
This expresses the actual product
capability and prevents a client from turning one

9
00:00:22.573 --> 00:00:25.350
endpoint into a launcher for every
installed utility.

10
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:28.811
A known absolute executable path is often
the clearest choice for a

11
00:00:28.891 --> 00:00:32.513
security-sensitive service because it
avoids ambiguous PATH lookup.

12
00:00:32.593 --> 00:00:36.397
A bare program name may resolve
differently across development machines,

13
00:00:36.477 --> 00:00:39.254
containers, service accounts, and
production hosts.

14
00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:43.138
Search order can be affected by
environment variables and platform rules,

15
00:00:43.218 --> 00:00:46.357
and an unexpected binary with the same
name may be found first.

16
00:00:46.437 --> 00:00:48.792
Absolute paths are not universally
required,

17
00:00:48.872 --> 00:00:53.098
but the deployment must define and protect
whatever resolution mechanism it uses.

18
00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:57.163
Do not validate arbitrary executable
strings by checking their characters or

19
00:00:57.243 --> 00:00:57.846
extension.

20
00:00:57.926 --> 00:01:01.790
A syntactically ordinary path can still
identify an unauthorized program.

21
00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.829
Instead, configure approved paths outside
request data,

22
00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:07.323
verify deployment ownership and
permissions,

23
00:01:07.403 --> 00:01:09.758
and map supported operations to those
values.

24
00:01:09.838 --> 00:01:12.736
If portability requires platform-specific
locations,

25
00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:16.620
select them from trusted configuration at
startup and fail closed when the

26
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.443
configured executable is missing or
outside the expected installation.

27
00:01:20.523 --> 00:01:22.817
Avoid falling back silently to a PATH
search.

28
00:01:22.897 --> 00:01:25.976
Executable trust also includes
interpreters and wrappers.

29
00:01:26.056 --> 00:01:30.403
Launching a trusted runtime with a
request-selected script merely moves executable

30
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:31.932
selection into an argument.

31
00:01:32.012 --> 00:01:36.117
Package scripts, plugins, and
configuration files can carry equivalent power.

32
00:01:36.197 --> 00:01:39.337
Review every input that determines what
code the child loads,

33
00:01:39.417 --> 00:01:41.288
not only the first process argument.

34
00:01:41.368 --> 00:01:45.655
A narrow launcher binds one operation to
one approved program and a defined argument

35
00:01:45.735 --> 00:01:49.780
grammar, making both security review and
deployment verification tractable.
